#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059518 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98fb5d45-5215-506c-8b2a-99aaadb6631c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations With Linear Arguments"}] :question/id #uuid "3590f0e0-2ff9-58f4-8351-772b477c1cd2" :question/math-academy-id "e-4109" :question/problem "If $\\log_7 (2x + 1) = 1,$ then what is $x?$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(2x+1) & =1 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(2x+1)} & ={7}^{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $7^{\\log_7 (2x + 1)} = 2x+1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+1 & =7 \\\\ 2x & =6 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059592 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b024b05-1efe-594e-9a7e-6d51a28c0c78" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "7055ec96-b829-5cca-8149-314b4c8fc6c8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5111" :question/problem "Given that $2\\log_5 (x-3)+1 = 5,$ then what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make $\\log_5 (x-3)$ the subject of the equation by moving everything else to the right-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{log}_{5}⁡(x-3)+1 & =5 \\\\ 2{log}_{5}⁡(x-3) & =4 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(x-3) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use a base of $5$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(x-3) & =2 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(x-3)} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $5^{\\log_5 (x - 3)} = x-3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x-3 & =25 \\\\ x & =28\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fd76722-397f-564f-b592-52156e86ddc0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "77f6d553-3291-5110-a30e-11991ae88425" :question/math-academy-id "e-5118" :question/problem "If $\\log_2 x = 5,$ then what is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $2,$ so we use a base of $2$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{2}⁡x & =5 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡x} & ={2}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $2^{\\log_2 x} = x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={2}^{5} \\\\ x & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "310db608-6d8b-51af-8abd-60f4dbd05423" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "bd3c7932-a6eb-5de6-a485-84e5d44656a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-5119" :question/problem "If $7\\log_6{x} - 3\\log_6{x} = 2,$ then what is the value of  $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify our equation by collecting the like logarithmic terms, and isolating the logarithm, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{log}_{6}⁡x-3{log}_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ (7-3){log}_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ 4{log}_{6}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {log}_{6}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $6,$ so we use a base of $6$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{6}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {6}^{{log}_{6}⁡x} & ={6}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we know that $6^{\\log_6 x} = x.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={6}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98fb5d45-5215-506c-8b2a-99aaadb6631c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3eaaba8c-471d-518c-a335-8c5cde9e9c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3293" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525360} {:answer/id #uuid "65eaf07e-bdf4-53cf-8ef4-ced6c5a60ccb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3893" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525466} {:answer/id #uuid "6ade2813-1c5a-5d24-911e-219441ba006c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2401" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525480} {:answer/id #uuid "732c204b-256d-58ac-94af-cde5a02cfff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2398" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525498} {:answer/id #uuid "9323dee0-34d8-5539-a4fe-1ad547c359d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2404" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9323dee0-34d8-5539-a4fe-1ad547c359d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2404" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525578} :answer-field/id #uuid "19b29739-e711-5183-b197-e37019643638" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9324f898-b949-55cf-ad73-91b42fed29fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-100563" :question/problem "If ${log}_{7}⁡(a-3)=4$, then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $7,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{7}⁡(a-3) & =4 \\\\ {7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(a-3)} & ={7}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${7}^{{log}_{7}⁡(a-3)}=a-3.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-3 & ={7}^{4} \\\\ a-3 & =2401 \\\\ a & =2404.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526104 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98fb5d45-5215-506c-8b2a-99aaadb6631c" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations With Linear Arguments"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "098dba23-b7da-562c-9c53-d2da5ac0e510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "133" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525228} {:answer/id #uuid "125fe57b-d15c-5087-b2fa-3310763c518c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525245} {:answer/id #uuid "3eec9cbe-47d1-5073-9d62-2079bb3fe062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "153" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525362} {:answer/id #uuid "680fe524-1b0c-58a3-9d0c-3b29c4e5eede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "103" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525469} {:answer/id #uuid "9f4ba5b7-2c58-52f0-a9a1-7fba8055f1b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "125fe57b-d15c-5087-b2fa-3310763c518c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525245} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b9c11fc-eb88-5bce-941d-5218b2921e27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa2d3b27-4298-5b73-9f1c-ae2a2ae60004" :question/math-academy-id "q-100565" :question/problem "If ${log}_{5}⁡(a-10)=3$, then $a=$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(a-10) & =3 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(a-10)} & ={5}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(a-10)}=a-10.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a-10 & ={5}^{3} \\\\ a-10 & =125 \\\\ a & =135.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b024b05-1efe-594e-9a7e-6d51a28c0c78" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "085d1057-ff5e-5bfd-84ae-c6c267e09ee0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "701" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525226} {:answer/id #uuid "4ca4cf8a-ca07-5e32-99ff-1650a91e4c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "753" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525398} {:answer/id #uuid "ae15ec67-0b24-5512-880c-5ce8aff444ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "720" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525647} {:answer/id #uuid "e417d031-50a9-529b-8959-d40b2f8ee096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "729" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525771} {:answer/id #uuid "fadffd7b-da29-5db1-a407-909c2c22fc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "705" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fadffd7b-da29-5db1-a407-909c2c22fc13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "705" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525828} :answer-field/id #uuid "f097babc-302d-5abc-8e66-b8adbc6bdc3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a40236b-6da0-50ec-9ab9-4d1b2b103597" :question/math-academy-id "q-100612" :question/problem "If $\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}{3}=2$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}{3} & =2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x+24) & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $3,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(x+24) & =6 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)} & ={3}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x+24)}=x+24.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+24 & ={3}^{6} \\\\ x+24 & =729 \\\\ x & =705.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "310db608-6d8b-51af-8abd-60f4dbd05423" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ef75135-19a4-52a8-ad6d-a258b56de06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525363} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3ff2ff-ba42-5ab5-8146-22b30c6d4fd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525389} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac4b53a-68f0-5fef-800d-e0192121b32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525552} {:answer/id #uuid "941a4670-57a3-5a46-81db-969321edf9a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525585} {:answer/id #uuid "daa3c953-83ea-5e0d-add7-38558f47a086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ac4b53a-68f0-5fef-800d-e0192121b32e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525552} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cfcf2f0-782a-59a2-a6ca-6394fc9db061" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525908}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c83d3104-e874-508c-b1b2-d01d79f86eaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-100631" :question/problem "If ${log}_{8}⁡x+2{log}_{8}⁡x=2$, then what is the value of  $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡x+2{log}_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ (1+2){log}_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ 3{log}_{8}⁡x & =2 \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $8,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{8}⁡x & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ {8}^{{log}_{8}⁡x} & ={8}^{2/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${8}^{{log}_{8}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={8}^{2/3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt[3]{64} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526019 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "310db608-6d8b-51af-8abd-60f4dbd05423" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations by Combining Logarithmic Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01087968-b5e0-5e77-8046-9109c861fbee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525211} {:answer/id #uuid "342d5133-1d9b-5d7c-b9e7-094c04737cad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525336} {:answer/id #uuid "61bdedb0-a0de-5b58-811f-ca4c7de81d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525453} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dfaa4a-44ef-50c8-ad65-b5ad3e047b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525671} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f9b357-1256-533e-a3ec-63e4e9ef8974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4dfaa4a-44ef-50c8-ad65-b5ad3e047b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525671} :answer-field/id #uuid "d74c0c25-3def-5bbb-a734-c7a9a9a0dc1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "542b7bfd-761e-58a3-80e1-933f25032116" :question/math-academy-id "q-110771" :question/problem "If $4{log}_{9}⁡x-2{log}_{9}⁡x=1,$ then what is $x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{log}_{9}⁡x-2{log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ (4-2){log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ 2{log}_{9}⁡x & =1 \\\\ {log}_{9}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $9,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {9}^{{log}_{9}⁡x} & ={9}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${9}^{{log}_{9}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={9}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fd76722-397f-564f-b592-52156e86ddc0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96f60cea-d0ed-5e57-b776-5f23c8cb82cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525593} {:answer/id #uuid "a14f080b-5b79-54e6-bf53-74347cda080c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525619} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c53b31-26fe-5859-a29b-b9971753aace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525623} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e68f27-92d0-5e53-b5d3-e8b567498210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525634} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c7f516-6829-5836-adff-04dc38af5587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96f60cea-d0ed-5e57-b776-5f23c8cb82cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525593} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c758b8-da8e-5d7a-b4de-50e6b1992bbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5e67b4cc-0d2e-51f0-a128-4fd093eea642" :question/math-academy-id "q-25563" :question/problem "If ${log}_{36}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2},$ then what is $x?$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $36,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{36}⁡x & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {36}^{{log}_{36}⁡x} & ={36}^{1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${36}^{{log}_{36}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={36}^{1/2} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\ x & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186526082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b024b05-1efe-594e-9a7e-6d51a28c0c78" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations by First Rearranging the Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d69e3c-ff46-5462-b1dc-f842432f8c4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525283} {:answer/id #uuid "30fa87c3-0fc0-5d04-8956-098ecc804898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525323} {:answer/id #uuid "69591349-2fae-5161-83b5-ca206ebd1db3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525473} {:answer/id #uuid "ea5cf105-98f1-5ff9-965a-e6cdd361c8d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525786} {:answer/id #uuid "ff49e680-ff34-505a-abda-dc9a48e3fc8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30fa87c3-0fc0-5d04-8956-098ecc804898" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525323} :answer-field/id #uuid "f060ff52-77ea-5902-887f-7649c45ec084" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d546754a-f994-5144-a7ef-1e397b7589a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-79381" :question/problem "If $3{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)=6$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =6 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(x+3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =2 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)}=x+2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & =25 \\\\ x & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fd76722-397f-564f-b592-52156e86ddc0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Logarithmic Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ee631ca-08b6-57a2-baf8-01c55b9afe77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059221} {:answer/id #uuid "c5af626c-0f95-5482-8308-f1c6ab338191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059222} {:answer/id #uuid "28ce4cae-57d3-5a1a-b387-e5356ff9eb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059223} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6093c7-d7d3-5b73-8456-ffe862656b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059224} {:answer/id #uuid "4a120f6a-8cf8-5a36-8599-d3bf2ba42ffa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059225}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ee631ca-08b6-57a2-baf8-01c55b9afe77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059221} :answer-field/id #uuid "dc9fc087-b031-5720-bd66-af06b92f0136" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186059220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c23322a3-0d32-5f5e-94fe-517b1c9adee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-906" :question/problem "If ${log}_{4}⁡x=3$, then $x=$" :question/worked-solution "To remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $4,$ so we use a base of $4$ for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{4}⁡x & =3 \\\\ {4}^{{log}_{4}⁡x} & ={4}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${4}^{{log}_{4}⁡x}=x.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={4}^{3} \\\\ x & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
